  "lens": {
   "mechanics": "Gold competes with cash and bonds as a store of wealth. When the return on bonds after inflation falls, or the dollar loses buying power, holding gold costs less and demand rises. When money tightens, the reverse happens.",
   "data": "Three forces. First, inflation is still building: consumer prices are up 3.7% y/y and import prices rose 1.1% in a month. Second, the real 10-year yield slipped to 2.74% from 2.78%, still a high real return but a slightly smaller headwind. Third, breakeven inflation edged up to 2.35%, so expected inflation rose as real yields fell. The template says tightening into a slowdown later turns to easing. Today differs: hiring has stalled at +29k, yet inflation is rising, so markets still price a December hike.",
   "unsustainable": "Interest takes about 21% of federal revenue (Q2 2026), like a family whose loan bill grows faster than its pay. Each hike adds to it. These are rough indicators.",
