   "cycle": "Debt keeps growing faster than revenue, now 6.5 times a year's revenue. The big change over the year is real rates: the real 10-year yield went from 2.2 points below real growth to 0.6 points above it, the condition under which debt burdens grow on their own. Fast nominal growth keeps the ratios steady for now. That reads as late in the cycle, in a tightening phase rather than a printing one.",
   "asset_classes": "While real yields are high, cash earns a real return. Long-term bonds carry the risk that heavy debts are later eased with printing. Stocks depend on growth holding up under tighter money. Gold has tended to do best in the printing phase, not the tightening phase, so the framework treats it as a diversifier, not a timing call."
